<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Win11 strikes again...  or not?<br><br>A couple of days ago, I attempted to update the firmware on both my GD-77 and my MD-9600. My last successful update was on or about August 19. The GD-77 went without a hitch. The MD-9600, when placed in firmware update mode (blinking LCD) generated this error in the CPS:  "radio not connected or not in DFU mode." <br><br>I checked the cable and USB port by restarting the radio and downloading the code plug to the CPS. Worked fine. I checked Device Manager for an unknown device, couldn't find one. There were a couple of entries in the USB controllers section for devices that had failed to register, so I uninstalled them. I reinstalled the drivers that had worked up until then, at:<br><br>"c:\Program Files (x86)\STMicroelectronics\Software\DfuSe v3.0.6\Bin\Driver\Win10\dpinst_amd64.exe".<br><br>And I rebooted the PC.  Nothing worked. I gave up and went to bed. <br><br>The next day, I tried again. Again, I confirmed the cable and USB port by downloading the code plug from the radio, then switched the radio to DFU mode. This time, the firmware upgrade worked perfectly. The only thing I did differently was to use a different USB port. But the original port had successfully downloaded the code plug the day before, had successfully updated both radios in the past. And I never saw the telltale entry of "STM Device in DFU mode" in Device Manager, USB Controllers section. <br><br>Go figure...</div></blockquote>Windows seems to install a driver per USB port. Mostly it installs the same driver as on the other USB ports, but I guess it may be technically possible to have different DFU drivers on the different USB ports.<br><br><br>Windows 11 is a nightmare for updating drivers without asking whether its allowed to do this. (W10 may be the same but the problem hardly every occurs, whereas W11 it happens a lot)<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Tue Oct 22, 2024 10:25 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
