		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=2573&amp;p=24678#p24678"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div><blockquote class="uncited"><div>This is a problem as more users move over to Windows 11.<br><br>Could it be possible to use drivers from the newer STM32CubeProgrammer if the CPS is modified for those API's ?</div></blockquote>CPS does not install DFU drivers. Windows does this.<br><br>It seems sometimes Windows installs very old drivers that may not work.<br><br>We can't distribute STM32 DFU Drivers because  AFIK the STM pacakage forbids this.<br><br>In that case people will need to download the DFU package from STM, which requires creating an account with STM and agreeing to the EULA etc<br><br>There is nothing we can do about the license on these drivers, and also which driver Windows installs.</div></blockquote>But it seems that no proper DFU driver exist for Windows 11 ? <br><br>So even if users download driver from STM there is a problem as the driver isn't compatible with Windows 11 unless you disable security functions in the OS.<br>Dfuse seems to be obsolete and replaced by STM32CubeProgrammer so I don't think that STM will update the old driver.<br><br>This will of course affect the Chinese original software too but I doubt that they will address the problem.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2867">SA0BUX</a> — Sat Jul 29, 2023 7:06 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
